---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Slip & Fall FAQ | slipfallcalculator.com';
const description = 'Frequently asked questions about slip and fall claims, settlements, and liability.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Slip &amp; Fall FAQ</h1>

  <h2>How long do I have to file a slip and fall claim?</h2>
  <p>Statutes of limitations vary by state — typically 2–3 years from the date of injury. Government property claims often have much shorter notice requirements (as little as 60–180 days). Consult an attorney promptly to protect your rights.</p>

  <h2>What must I prove to win a slip and fall case?</h2>
  <p>You must establish that: (1) the property owner owed you a duty of care; (2) they breached that duty by creating or allowing a hazardous condition; (3) the hazard caused your fall; and (4) you suffered damages as a result.</p>

  <h2>What is "notice" and why does it matter?</h2>
  <p>"Notice" means the property owner knew or should have known about the hazard. Actual notice means they were told directly; constructive notice means the hazard existed long enough that a reasonable owner should have discovered it. Without notice, liability is harder to establish.</p>

  <h2>How much is an average slip and fall settlement?</h2>
  <p>Minor injury cases often settle for $15,000–$50,000. Moderate injury cases (fractures, surgery) range from $50,000–$150,000. Severe or permanent injury cases can exceed $500,000. These figures vary widely by state, insurance coverage, and specific facts.</p>

  <h2>What if I was partly at fault for my fall?</h2>
  <p>Most states use comparative fault rules. In pure comparative fault states, you can recover even if 99% at fault (your damages are reduced proportionally). In modified comparative fault states, you're barred from recovery if you're 50% or 51% or more at fault depending on the state.</p>

  <h2>Should I accept the first settlement offer?</h2>
  <p>Usually no. Initial offers from insurance adjusters are typically low. You should understand the full extent of your injuries and have legal representation before accepting any settlement.</p>
</BaseLayout>
